# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The audit in antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197 went
# looking for required-check lists that had drifted away from the checks CI
# actually produces. This repository's had not: the six names it then declared
# — `fmt`, `qemu-validation`, `test / ubuntu-latest`, `test / macos-latest`,
# `test / windows-latest` and `test (release)` — were exactly the six check-runs
# main's HEAD produced and exactly the six the newest pull_request check-suite
# produced, all `success`. Nothing needed correcting. It was declared anyway,
# because that list lived only in the GitHub settings page, and that page has no
# diff, no reviewer and no history anyone reads. The sibling rust-img-qcow2 is
# what the gap looks like after the fact: it produces a sixth check,
# `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`, that nobody ever made required, and the only
# reason we know is that somebody went and counted.
#
# ONE NAME NOW, BECAUSE A JOB STANDS FOR THE REST (#105). Declaring the six
# real names moved the list into a reviewable file but did not stop it drifting
# — it only made the drift a diff nobody was obliged to write. Both failure
# directions were still one ordinary edit away:
#
#   * add a fourth platform to ci.yml's `os:` matrix and its leg reports on
#     every pull request, requiring nothing, gating nothing. Green or red makes
#     no difference to the merge, which is the rust-img-qcow2 state again.
#   * rename a job, or mis-spell one here, and the name declared is a name no
#     job produces. GitHub reads a required-and-absent context as PENDING, not
#     as failed, so with enforce_admins on nothing merges and there is no
#     failure to point at. `test / release` for `test (release)` is the spelling
#     that does it: the matrix legs are "name, space, slash, space, matrix
#     value" because ci.yml's `name:` types it that way, while `test (release)`
#     is a job whose own `name:` carries literal parentheses and no slash.
#     Neither is a GitHub convention, so neither can be inferred from the other.
#
# So ci.yml grew one job, `ci-ok`, whose `needs:` names every other job in the
# file and which fails unless each concluded `success`. That list lives beside
# the jobs it names, in the same file, changed in the same diff that renames,
# splits or adds one — and the name protection asks for does not move when the
# jobs do.
#
# WHAT ci-ok STANDS FOR. All four jobs run on `pull_request` (ci.yml triggers on
# `push` to main and on `pull_request` with no branch filter), so all four can
# gate one:
#
#   test            three matrix legs, ubuntu/macos/windows-latest. Debug
#                   profile, so overflow checks are on; this is the run that
#                   carries the EXPECT_OVERFLOW_CHECKS handshake that
#                   tests/ci_profile.rs insists on. Three platforms because the
#                   byte-level VHDX code is the kind that differs by target,
#                   and windows-latest is the leg most likely to be quietly
#                   dropped.
#   test-release    `test (release)`, the release-profile run. Not redundant
#                   beside the matrix: release lets an integer overflow wrap
#                   where debug panics, so a test guarding a wrapping
#                   computation passes the debug run for the wrong reason.
#   qemu-validation cross-validates our reader/writer against `qemu-img`. The
#                   only job that proves we agree with an implementation we did
#                   not write, and the only one that builds
#                   tests/qemu_validation.rs with its feature on.
#   fmt             `cargo fmt --check`.
#
# NOTHING ELSE IN .github/workflows CAN GATE, and `needs:` could not reach it
# anyway — it only names jobs in its own file. release.yml triggers on a
# `v*.*.*` tag, which happens after the merge it would have gated, and fuzz.yml
# is workflow_dispatch plus a nightly cron. Neither reports on a pull request,
# and a required check that never reports blocks the branch permanently, which
# is the same pending-forever failure as a mis-spelled name.
#
# Third-party reviewers do not need excluding by hand: github-protect-main.sh
# filters `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")` on every query, so a review app
# cannot become required.
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which for this job is
# both its key and its `name:` — `ci-ok`.
#
# tests/ci_aggregate_gate.rs holds both halves to each other: every job in
# ci.yml has to appear in `ci-ok`'s `needs`, and this file has to require
# `ci-ok` and nothing else.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
